### What is a Kafka transaction?

A Kafka transaction groups a set of producer sends and an offset commit into one atomic unit. You start it, send records to any number of topics, optionally send consumed offsets, and commit or abort. If you commit, all records become visible and offsets advance; if you abort, nothing happens.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class TransactionalProducerDemo {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> template;

    public TransactionalProducerDemo(KafkaTemplate<String, String> template) {
        this.template = template;
    }

    public void transferFunds() {
        template.executeInTransaction(t -> {
            t.send("debit.events", "SAV2001", "{\"amount\":50000}");
            t.send("credit.events", "SAV3001", "{\"amount\":50000}");
            // Both sends commit together or both abort
            return null;
        });
        System.out.println("Both debit and credit published atomically");
    }
}
```

```text
Both debit and credit published atomically
```

### How do you enable Kafka transactions in Spring?

Set `spring.kafka.producer.transaction-id-prefix` to any stable prefix. This makes KafkaTemplate transactional and registers a KafkaTransactionManager. Use @Transactional or executeInTransaction.

```properties
# application.properties
spring.kafka.producer.transaction-id-prefix=settlement-tx-
spring.kafka.consumer.isolation.level=read_committed
spring.kafka.consumer.properties.enable.auto.commit=false
```

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class DeclarativeTransactionDemo {
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> template;

    public DeclarativeTransactionDemo(KafkaTemplate<String, String> template) {
        this.template = template;
    }

    @Transactional  // Kafka transaction wraps the whole method
    public void processAndPublish() {
        template.send("settlements", "SAV2001", "{\"status\":\"settled\"}");
        template.send("notifications", "SAV2001", "{\"msg\":\"Transfer complete\"}");
        // Both commit on method return, or both abort on exception
    }
}
```

```text
# On success: both records visible to read_committed consumers
# On exception: both records invisible, offsets unchanged
```

### How does read-process-write achieve exactly-once?

The consumer reads a record, processes it, produces output, and the consumed offset is included in the same transaction as the produced output. Either the output is produced and the offset advances, or neither happens.

```text
Read-process-write pipeline:
1. Consumer reads from input-topic (offset 42)
2. Process: transform the record
3. Producer sends to output-topic
4. Consumer offset 42 is sent to the Kafka transaction
5. Transaction COMMIT: output visible + offset advances to 43
   or ABORT: output invisible + offset stays at 42 (re-read)

Downstream consumers read output-topic with isolation.level=read_committed
```

### What is zombie fencing?

The transactional ID enables zombie fencing. A restarted producer gets a new epoch, and the broker rejects writes from the old stale instance. This prevents a hung former leader from corrupting the transaction.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class ZombieFencingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Producer-A starts with txn-id='settlement-tx-0', epoch=1");
        System.out.println("Producer-A hangs (network partition)");
        System.out.println("Producer-B starts with same txn-id, gets epoch=2");
        System.out.println("Producer-A tries to write -> broker rejects (stale epoch)");
        System.out.println("Only Producer-B (latest epoch) can write -> no corruption");
    }
}
```

```text
Producer-A starts with txn-id='settlement-tx-0', epoch=1
Producer-A hangs (network partition)
Producer-B starts with same txn-id, gets epoch=2
Producer-A tries to write -> broker rejects (stale epoch)
Only Producer-B (latest epoch) can write -> no corruption
```

### Why can't Kafka transactions span a database?

A Kafka transaction is confined to Kafka. There is no shared two-phase commit coordinator between the Kafka broker and a relational database. A DB write and a Kafka publish wrapped in the same method are NOT atomic.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class DualWriteProblemDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: dual write -- not atomic across DB and Kafka
        System.out.println("1. DB: debit Rajesh's account");
        System.out.println("2. Kafka: send TransferCompleted event");
        System.out.println("CRASH between step 1 and step 2!");
        System.out.println("Result: DB debited but event never published");
        System.out.println("Downstream services never hear about the transfer");
    }
}
```

```text
1. DB: debit Rajesh's account
2. Kafka: send TransferCompleted event
CRASH between step 1 and step 2!
Result: DB debited but event never published
Downstream services never hear about the transfer
```

### How does the transactional outbox pattern solve the dual write?

In the same local DB transaction that does the business change, insert the event into an outbox table. A separate relay (CDC like Debezium or a poller) reads the outbox and publishes to Kafka.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    @Transactional  // local DB transaction only
    public void transfer(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Business tables
        accountRepository.debit(fromAccount, amount);
        accountRepository.credit(toAccount, amount);

        // Outbox table -- in the SAME DB transaction
        outboxRepository.save(new OutboxEvent(
            "TransferCompleted",
            fromAccount,
            "{\"from\":\"" + fromAccount + "\",\"to\":\"" + toAccount
                + "\",\"amount\":" + amount + "}"
        ));
        // Debit, credit, and outbox row commit TOGETHER atomically
    }
}
// Relay (Debezium CDC) reads outbox table and publishes to Kafka
```

```text
DB transaction: debit SAV2001 + credit SAV3001 + outbox insert -> COMMIT
Relay: reads outbox row -> publishes to Kafka -> marks row sent
```

### When do you use Kafka transactions vs the outbox?

Kafka transactions are right when both the input and output are Kafka topics. The outbox is right whenever a database is one of the two systems.

| Scenario | Solution |
|---|---|
| Kafka topic -> process -> Kafka topic | Kafka transaction (exactly-once within Kafka) |
| DB write + Kafka publish | Transactional outbox (atomicity via local DB transaction) |
| DB write + DB write | Local DB transaction (standard ACID) |

```bash
# Rule of thumb:
# Both ends Kafka? -> Kafka transaction
# One end is a database? -> Transactional outbox
# Both ends databases? -> Local or distributed DB transaction
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The outbox relay is at-least-once: a crash after publishing but before marking the row sent causes a republish. Consumers must be idempotent, keyed by event ID. Downstream consumers of Kafka transactions must use `isolation.level=read_committed` to see only committed output. Zombie fencing protects against stale producers but adds a small overhead per transaction.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class OutboxRelayGuaranteeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Relay publishes outbox row to Kafka");
        System.out.println("CRASH before marking row as sent");
        System.out.println("Restart: relay publishes the same row AGAIN");
        System.out.println("Consumer must be idempotent (primary key = event_id)");
        System.out.println("Trade-off: guaranteed eventual delivery with tiny latency");
    }
}
```

```text
Relay publishes outbox row to Kafka
CRASH before marking row as sent
Restart: relay publishes the same row AGAIN
Consumer must be idempotent (primary key = event_id)
Trade-off: guaranteed eventual delivery with tiny latency
```
